Fixed. Here's the summary:

## Bug

`extractTags(from:)` split text by spaces and filtered tokens that *start* with `#`. Tags preceded by any non-space punctuation (`,`, `(`, `)`, `.`, etc.) were silently dropped:

- `"shopping list,#urgent"` → split gives `"list,#urgent"` → doesn't start with `#` → dropped
- `"(#ideas)"` → split gives `"(#ideas)"` → doesn't start with `#` → dropped

## Fix (in `NoteKit.swift` lines 4–24)

Replaced the space-split approach with a scan for `#` anywhere in the text. After finding `#`, it collects the maximal run of valid tag characters (`[a-zA-Z0-9_-]`) until a non-tag character is hit. This handles tags preceded by any punctuation while preserving order and existing behavior.

**Verified cases (Python equivalent, since Swift compilation was sandbox-blocked):**

| Input | Result | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `"hello #world #swift"` | `["world", "swift"]` | PASS (existing) |
| `"just plain text"` | `[]` | PASS (existing) |
| `"shopping list,#urgent"` | `["urgent"]` | PASS (bug fix) |
| `"(#ideas)"` | `["ideas"]` | PASS (bug fix) |
| `#standalone, #tag-with-dash, #tag_under, #123, #a#b` | all correct | PASS |


